# PHASE 3: EXECUTIVE AUTHENTICATION — ONE LOGIN METHOD ONLY

The current application has an inconsistency:

The default executive password is reportedly not working reliably, while the PIN fallback is functioning.

There must be exactly ONE executive authentication method.

Use:

**Individual username + individual password.**

Remove the PIN fallback after a safe credential migration.

## 3.1 One-time migration from the existing login

Do not immediately disable the only currently working administrative access method before establishing a new administrator account.

Use the existing working legacy access only as a temporary, authorised bootstrap mechanism.

The process must be:

1. Authenticate the current administrator through the working legacy mechanism once.
2. Create or confirm the first real system administrator account.
3. Assign the administrator's actual position.
4. Set a new individual username.
5. Set a strong individual password.
6. Confirm that the new username/password login works.
7. Revoke the legacy PIN mechanism.
8. Remove the shared default password.
9. Invalidate obsolete shared credentials and old sessions.
10. Require all other officers to activate or reset their own credentials.

The legacy PIN must not remain as an alternative login after migration.

Do not hardcode old credentials into the new login screen.

Do not display the old PIN in source code comments or public interface messages.

## 3.2 Individual executive credentials

Every executive must have:

- Unique Executive ID.
- Unique username.
- Individual password.
- Actual full name.
- One assigned primary position.
- Approved permissions.
- Account status.

No shared executive password.

No universal default password.

Do not prepopulate fake executive names.

## 3.3 Remove role switching completely

The current executive interface allows switching roles.

This must be removed.

When an individual logs in:

- The backend identifies their account.
- The backend identifies their assigned official position.
- Their permissions are loaded automatically.
- Their dashboard displays their actual position.

Example:

